A||N||7||Mattsfiero||Another frozen Bolt||||4||
Z||000000||Mattsfiero||04-15-2001||07:42 PM||||Well I started the Dubious Task of a Clutch on a 2.8 V6 and well so far it has been the worst yet. Well here is the major thing. We had a rear cradle bolt that was froxen but got past it but the one in the back (Side ways ) as we all know it runs through the bushing. well the metal colar in the bushing has frozen to the bolt and th bushing to the cradle. So when you try to turn it it feels as though you are tightening some major spring. Any Ideas on how to free it? <P>Thanks Matt<P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@aol.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||4
Z||000001||FieroBUZZ||04-15-2001||07:57 PM||||Matt:  <BR>Join the crowd, never had to do a clutch in my life till I got a Fiero.  I got the back bolts free but am still soaking the front ones.  It's pretty rusty there.  Hopefully they come out okay.  Good luck!<BR>Gary<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||04-16-2001||07:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mattsfiero:<BR><B>Well I started the Dubious Task of a Clutch on a 2.8 V6 and well so far it has been the worst yet. Well here is the major thing. We had a rear cradle bolt that was froxen but got past it but the one in the back (Side ways ) as we all know it runs through the bushing. well the metal colar in the bushing has frozen to the bolt and th bushing to the cradle. So when you try to turn it it feels as though you are tightening some major spring. Any Ideas on how to free it? <P>Thanks Matt<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Time for the blue wrench...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||04-16-2001||08:37 PM||||Burn it baby burn||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mattsfiero||04-17-2001||07:25 AM||||Well Bill The torch had crossed my mind but the bolt is hidden in the frame for the most part and the heat would only melt the bushing and not loosen the bolt like I would like. Thanks any how. Any othe suggestions??<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@aol.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||3
Z||000005||thomas_l||04-17-2001||08:59 AM||||Get a die grinder and cut the head and nut off the bolt, then use prybars to get the cradle down. Get a replacement bushing and bolt and you're all set.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||04-17-2001||07:27 PM||||now is definatly time to replace the bushings.<P>There is a seam in the sleeve of the OE front cradel bushing. If you can see the seam, hit it with a center punch. this will slightly spread the seam and that usually gets it loose prettty quick. ((It's hard to see. you'll need a flashlight and maybe a bit of poking with the thin screwdriver to find it.))<P>If you can't find the seam try giving the sleeve a few whacks with a pin punch. That won't spread it but might knock it loose.<P><BR>If you are going to scrap the bushings, heat the bolt untill the rubber will tear loose from the sleeve as you turn the bolt. then find the seam and hit it with the center punch.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mattsfiero||04-17-2001||09:10 PM||||I will definately give that a try Ogre and let you know how it turns out.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@aol.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||10
